<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  What the App Store copy is allowed to do. The same least-privilege stance as
  capabilities/default.json, one layer down: the sandbox is on, and nothing is
  opened up beyond what the app cannot run without. The document lives in the
  app's own container, which a sandboxed app may always read and write, so no
  file entitlement is needed.

  The network entitlement is not about the app reaching the network — it never
  does. WKWebView loads even the app's own `tauri://` frontend through
  WebKit's networking process, and inside the sandbox that process is refused
  without it. Signed without this the window comes up blank: no frontend, so
  no persister, so no document written. It was A/B tested that way.

  The two identifiers pair the signature with the Mac App Store provisioning
  profile — they must match what that profile grants.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.application-identifier</key>
	<string>Q4C48EKH9Z.tech.median.milestones</string>
	<key>com.apple.developer.team-identifier</key>
	<string>Q4C48EKH9Z</string>
</dict>
</plist>
